1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,560
In this exercise, you are asked to implement a function that converts

2
00:00:04,560 --> 00:00:11,680
the TF record in the way more open data set format to a different TF record format.

3
00:00:12,710 --> 00:00:18,660
This function takes as input the file name of the original TF record,

4
00:00:18,660 --> 00:00:22,695
an encoded jpeg image in a byte like format,

5
00:00:22,695 --> 00:00:26,260
and the list containing annotations.

6
00:00:27,110 --> 00:00:31,500
First, we need to load the encoded image in

7
00:00:31,500 --> 00:00:35,550
a memory buffer using Python input-output library.

8
00:00:35,550 --> 00:00:41,950
We can then open this image with Pillow and extract the image widths and height.

9
00:00:42,780 --> 00:00:49,510
We use the provided class mapping to map classes name to classes IDs.

10
00:00:49,510 --> 00:00:54,520
Indeed, the way more open data set only contain the classes name.

11
00:00:54,520 --> 00:00:58,450
We then need to convert the annotation in the right format.

12
00:00:58,450 --> 00:01:00,775
In a way more open data sets,

13
00:01:00,775 --> 00:01:06,445
bounding boxes use the center coordinates instead of the corner coordinates.

14
00:01:06,445 --> 00:01:09,310
We convert them to the wanted format by

15
00:01:09,310 --> 00:01:13,735
adding or subtracting half of the lengths and half of the widths.

16
00:01:13,735 --> 00:01:16,930
Multiple bounding boxes format exist and

17
00:01:16,930 --> 00:01:20,780
this is an operation you will have to do pretty frequently.

18
00:01:21,210 --> 00:01:28,384
Finally, we need to create the different protocol messages using the provided functions.

19
00:01:28,384 --> 00:01:33,109
We put these protocol messages into a TF train example.

20
00:01:33,109 --> 00:01:37,985
Because different machine learning libraries require different data formats,

21
00:01:37,985 --> 00:01:43,530
you need to become comfortable converting data from one format to another.

